title: 'Build context tar drops file modes: executables arrive in the image without
  +x'
updated: 2026-10-06T19:41:36Z
---

`Client::_create_tar` (`lib/Dist/Zilla/Plugin/Docker/API/Client.pm:297-321`) slurps every file and adds it with `$tar->add_data($name, $content)` and no options. Archive::Tar then gives every entry its default mode (0666 minus umask) and type "regular file". The build root Dist::Zilla wrote has the right modes (ExecDir makes `bin/*` 0755); the context the engine receives does not.

Reproduce:

    mkdir -p /tmp/ctx/bin /tmp/ctx/empty
    printf '#!/bin/sh\necho hi\n' > /tmp/ctx/bin/entry.sh; chmod 755 /tmp/ctx/bin/entry.sh
    echo x > /tmp/ctx/.dockerignore; ln -s bin/entry.sh /tmp/ctx/link.sh
    perl -Ilib -MDist::Zilla::Plugin::Docker::API::Client -MArchive::Tar -e '
      my $c=Dist::Zilla::Plugin::Docker::API::Client->new(logger=>sub{},logger_fatal=>sub{die @_});
      my $t=$c->_create_tar(shift,"Dockerfile"); open my $fh,"<",$t; my $tar=Archive::Tar->new($fh);
      printf "%-14s mode=%04o type=%s\n",$_->full_path,$_->mode,$_->type for $tar->get_files' /tmp/ctx
    # link.sh        mode=0664 type=0
    # bin/entry.sh   mode=0664 type=0

Four things are lost in one go:
- the executable bit (0755 -> 0664): `COPY entrypoint.sh ...` + `ENTRYPOINT` fails with "permission denied" unless the Dockerfile repairs it;
- symlinks are followed and stored as regular files (`_collect_files` uses `is_dir`/`slurp_raw`, `:327-333`); a dangling symlink makes `slurp_raw` die;
- every top-level dotfile is skipped (`next if $name =~ /^\./`, `:306`), including `.dockerignore`, while dotfiles one level down are kept;
- empty directories are not in the archive.

The fleet already works around the first one: `karr/Dockerfile:82`, `p5-mcp-wiki/Dockerfile:48` and `p5-mcp-hub/Dockerfile:86` each `RUN chmod +x` a file they just copied.

Done when the context keeps mode and symlinks (e.g. `Archive::Tar->add_files` on paths relative to the context, or `add_data` with `{ mode => ... }` and a symlink entry), the dotfile rule is either dropped or documented, and a test builds a context with an executable, a symlink and a dotfile and asserts on the tar entries. No test under `t/` looks at modes today.

